ex:abstract-body a ga:Fragment ; ga:subject ex:subject ; ga:format "text/markdown" ; ga:contribution ex:authored ;
    ga:body "Python, shell, JavaScript, TypeScript, JSON, JSON-LD, Turtle and SPARQL examples share the same syntax colours in HTML and PDF. Code is displayed as source and is never executed." .
ex:status-body a ga:Fragment ; ga:subject ex:subject ; ga:format "text/markdown" ; ga:contribution ex:authored ;
    ga:body "This executable showcase verifies offline publication from a restored documentation graph. All names and endpoints are illustrative." .
ex:model-body a ga:Fragment ; ga:subject ex:subject ; ga:format "text/markdown" ; ga:contribution ex:authored ;
    ga:body "Each fenced block retains its language and source text. The optional `example`\nmarker adds a numbered caption. Syntax colours are generated during publication.\n\n## Python\n\n```python example\nimport json\n\n# Unicode and multiline strings remain literal source.\ndef describe(name: str) -> str:\n    message = f\"\"\"Hello {name}\nA second line stays inside this string.\"\"\"\n    return json.dumps({\"name\": name, \"message\": message}, ensure_ascii=False)\n\nprint(describe(\"Bønds\"))\n```\n\n## Bash\n\n```bash example\n#!/bin/bash\n# Environment references and quoted backticks are displayed, never run.\nname=\"Bønds\"\nprintf '%s\\n' \"$name\" '$HOME' '`literal`'\ncurl --header 'Accept: application/ld+json' 'https://example.org/data'\n```\n\n## JavaScript and Node.js\n\n```javascript example\nconst name = \"Bønds\";\nconst description = `Hello ${name}`;\nconst record = { name, active: true, count: 42 };\nconsole.log(JSON.stringify(record));\n```\n\n## TypeScript\n\n```typescript example\ninterface DataProduct {\n  name: string;\n  active: boolean;\n}\nconst product: DataProduct = { name: \"UK Bonds\", active: true };\nconsole.log(product.name);\n```\n\n## JSON\n\n```json example\n{\n  \"name\": \"UK Bonds\",\n  \"active\": true,\n  \"count\": 42,\n  \"markup\": \"<script>& </code>\",\n  \"missing\": null\n}\n```\n\n## JSON-LD\n\n```json-ld example\n{\n  \"@context\": {\n    \"@vocab\": \"https://schema.org/\",\n    \"name\": { \"@id\": \"name\", \"@language\": \"en\" }\n  },\n  \"@id\": \"https://example.org/products/bonds\",\n  \"@type\": \"Dataset\",\n  \"name\": \"UK Bonds\"\n}\n```\n\n## SPARQL\n\n```sparql example\nPREFIX dct: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/>\nPREFIX dcat: <http://www.w3.org/ns/dcat#>\n\n# Both SPARQL variable spellings are supported.\nSELECT ?dataset $title WHERE {\n  ?dataset a dcat:Dataset ;\n           dct:title $title .\n  FILTER(LANG($title) = \"en\")\n}\nORDER BY $title\nLIMIT 20\n```\n\n## Turtle\n\n```turtle example\n@prefix ex: <https://example.org/> .\n@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .\n\n# A labelled dataset with a numeric count.\nex:bonds a ex:Dataset ;\n    ex:label \"UK Bonds\"@en ;\n    ex:count 42 ;\n    ex:active true .\n```\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> These examples are documentation, not executable build steps. Publication\n> needs no browser script, syntax service or additional Typst package download.\n" .

ex:publisher a <https://ekgf.org/ontology/artifact-governance#Authority> ; <https://ekgf.org/ontology/grapharch#slug> "example" ; <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Grapharch contributors"@en ; <https://ekgf.org/ontology/artifact-governance#authorityAcronym> "Grapharch" ; ga:accentColor "#2b5f9f" ; ga:url "https://ekgf.github.io/grapharch/" .
